WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:08.221 --> 00:00:13.221
♪ Born in 1966 ♪

2
00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:17.569
♪ He turned his life around ♪

3
00:00:17.569 --> 00:00:20.093
♪ He wasn't doing so hot ♪

4
00:00:20.093 --> 00:00:24.137
♪ Rich Roll ♪

5
00:00:24.137 --> 00:00:28.545
♪ He got rid of that weight and he gained a lot of wisdom ♪

6
00:00:28.545 --> 00:00:33.341
♪ Rich Roll ♪

7
00:00:33.341 --> 00:00:36.540
♪ He lives in a tent ♪

8
00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.873
♪ Rich Roll ♪

9
00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.410
Thank you all so much for playing along,

10
00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:47.650
and I hope you have a wonderful night.

11
00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:52.090
And I'd like to welcome to the stage, Mr. Rich Roll!

12
00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:59.800
(fast guitar strumming)

13
00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.200
Hey, everybody. Welcome to "Roll On".

14
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.340
We're an athlete/podcaster/writer,

15
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:11.340
and a journalist/author/mask-wearing shark lover,

16
00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:16.530 line:15%
rip headlines from the internet and real world life

17
00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:17.990 line:15%
and wax on matters of interest,

18
00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:19.730 line:15%
try to make sense of the world.

19
00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:23.170
Today, we've got a few interesting matters to unpack.

20
00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:26.890
But the thrust of today is celebrating

21
00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:29.060
that little book that you're reading right there, Adam.

22
00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:30.380
What are you reading?

23
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.480
Oh, oh, oh, oh, are we on?

24
00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.060
Is this live? Is this thing live?

25
00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.110
Is this the hot mic?

26
00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:35.960
This is.

27
00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.210
I'm reading this great memoir; it's captivating.

28
00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.970
I think you should check it out.

29
00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:45.070
Actually you're reading the first version

30
00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:46.990
before I revised it.
First edition, baby.

31
00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:48.550
You need to read this one, I think.

32
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:50.130
Oh really, is that the better version?

33
00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:51.990
This is better, yes.

34
00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.990
So I have the first version and it's signed by the author.

35
00:01:57.687 --> 00:01:59.520
"Adam, let's hit the point soon.

36
00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:01.640
Thanks for being you, my friend."

37
00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.660
Is that the one that I gave you

38
00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.027
when we did our first podcast together?

39
00:02:05.027 --> 00:02:06.140
It is, it is.

40
00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:09.720
I had a Kindle copy, and then you sent me this one,

41
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:10.600
or you gave me this one.

42
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:11.930
I'm glad that you still have it.

43
00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:13.090
Yeah, man.

44
00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:17.010
When I did the New York Times story on you for a backstory,

45
00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:20.403
yes, I wrote a Rich Roll in New York Times sports story,

46
00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.610
I reread it and love it, of course.

47
00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:25.730
I appreciate that.
Yeah, man.

48
00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:27.930
You being on "Roll On" is just me paying you back

49
00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.070
for that New York Times story.

50
00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:30.600
That was the deal that we struck.

51
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.301
Has it been that useful for you?

52
00:02:33.301 --> 00:02:35.690
(both laugh)

53
00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:36.790
No, it was great.

54
00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.007
That was in the wake of Otillo.

55
00:02:39.916 --> 00:02:41.557
Otillo.
Otillo.

56
00:02:41.557 --> 00:02:43.210
Yeah, no, it came out in the wake of it.

57
00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:45.270
Right, yeah, but we did the research prior.

58
00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:47.400
That would never made the print edition, though.

59
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.060
Listen, man--
I'm still grinding on that.

60
00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:50.454
I just work here.

61
00:02:50.454 --> 00:02:51.287
(both laugh)
I know.

62
00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:53.910
Anyway, good to see you, my friend.

63
00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:55.370
Good to see you.

64
00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:57.230
Glad to be back in the podcast studio with you.

65
00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:59.920
And yes, we're gonna talk about "Finding Ultra".

66
00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.290
Why are we talking about "Finding Ultra"?

67
00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:05.420
Because this week marks the 10th anniversary of this book.

68
00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.010
So I thought it would be fun--

69
00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:07.843
Congratulations.

70
00:03:07.843 --> 00:03:09.413
To celebrate that a little bit,

71
00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.470
have a little peek through the rear view.

72
00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.610
I'm having trouble talking today.

73
00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.400
Anyway, yeah, we're gonna have a little discussion

74
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.030
about the art of memoir and just writing in general

75
00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.880
and I think it's gonna be cool.

76
00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:28.560
But before that, how goes it, my friend?

77
00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:29.480
It's good.

78
00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:30.950
I've been--

79
00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:32.410
So you just said you were good.

80
00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:33.243
I know you.

81
00:03:33.243 --> 00:03:34.760
You said, you think people are complaining

82
00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.110
that every time I ask you how you're doing

83
00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:36.943
you say you're good.

84
00:03:36.943 --> 00:03:38.290
No, they did complain.
Then you just did it again.

85
00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:39.230
They did complain.

86
00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:42.793
And then I came up with my high jinks, my intro high jinks.

87
00:03:44.529 --> 00:03:47.056
But then the intro high jinks got--

88
00:03:47.056 --> 00:03:47.889
You just pivoted back to your--

89
00:03:47.889 --> 00:03:49.790
Yeah, so then I went back to my muscle.

90
00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.860
But to be honest--
How are you doing really?

91
00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.210
If you really wanna know,

92
00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:57.600
I've been deep in the deadline cave

93
00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.770
and I have a deadline beard to show for it.

94
00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:01.252
You do.
If you're not watching.

95
00:04:01.252 --> 00:04:03.440
I think you should just continue with the beard.

96
00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.370
Now it's moved past the growing down and out

97
00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:09.850
and it's moved into the round phase.

98
00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:14.550
So I'm dangerously close to having a half circle of hair

99
00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:16.730
that just arcs around my face.

100
00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:17.563
Yeah, I know the feeling.

101
00:04:17.563 --> 00:04:18.960
That's why I just shaved mine off,

102
00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.500
'cause it was in Chia Pet stage.

103
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:22.333
Yeah, that's where I'm at.

104
00:04:22.333 --> 00:04:23.693
It's jowly. It's real jowly.

105
00:04:23.693 --> 00:04:25.660
It gets good when it gets long enough

106
00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:28.210
where you can kinda bring it down.

107
00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:29.760
But that's a full commitment.

108
00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:30.900
When I shaved mine the other day,

109
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.440
I thought about keeping the mustache.

110
00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:34.670
I was this close.

111
00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:36.270
That would've been pretty--
And I chickened out.

112
00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:37.540
I'm actually thankful

113
00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.110
I'm not staring at a mustache right now.

114
00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.070
That would be hard to--
It's distracting.

115
00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:42.529
Nobody needs that.

116
00:04:42.529 --> 00:04:44.670
(Adam laughs)

117
00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.840
But this is a deadline beard.

118
00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:50.600
It's kind of like a cousin to the hockey playoff beard.

119
00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:53.850
Shout out to my brothers and sisters up in Canada.

120
00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:57.800
Not that there's anything wrong with playoff beards.

121
00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:58.660
I like playoff beards.

122
00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:01.010
I used it once in Nicaragua

123
00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.080
when I was on Lonely Planet jag,

124
00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.960
a deep, I've done like back to back to back

125
00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:07.300
Lonely Planet assignments

126
00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.100
and the last one was in Nicaragua and they,

127
00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.430
it was me and one other writer,

128
00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:15.100
and he lived in Guatemala so he had first choice

129
00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:18.260
and he picked all the places that tourists actually go,

130
00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:21.480
and then three quarters of the geographic region

131
00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:25.330
of Nicaragua where tourists never go he gave to me.

132
00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:29.080
So I was like, literally, like in the jungles.

133
00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:30.050
I mean, it was great stuff.

134
00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:32.060
I loved being out there, but like I don't know

135
00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:35.210
that anybody will ever have used my material for,

136
00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:37.930
maybe Peace Corps volunteers have used it,

137
00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:40.963
but by the time I got to the jungles,

138
00:05:42.581 --> 00:05:46.130
I was like so whacked that, like, I just couldn't shave.

139
00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:47.730
I had like, my nails were growing.

140
00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:49.420
I was like a beast.

141
00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:52.440
And I showed up in a Costa Rica hotel

142
00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:56.420
two days before my flight home and like Christmas time

143
00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:59.840
and went to the check-in desk and they thought that,

144
00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.310
they did not look happy to see me.

145
00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:04.109
Right, do you have photographic evidence of this?

146
00:06:04.109 --> 00:06:05.916
I don't know. I think I must.

147
00:06:05.916 --> 00:06:06.840
You should throw that out on Instagram.

148
00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:07.720
I must, I must.

149
00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.800
On Thursday when this goes out as a companion piece.

150
00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.110
Well, it's--
I need a visual.

151
00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:14.520
Yeah, it's 10 years since I've joined Instagram.

152
00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.410
So we both have 10-year--

153
00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.896
Congratulations on being--

154
00:06:18.896 --> 00:06:21.313
(both laugh)

155
00:06:22.203 --> 00:06:23.446
I thought you'd like that.

156
00:06:23.446 --> 00:06:25.270
We can all lament that.

157
00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:27.900
But I have been just writing

158
00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:31.170
and making oatmeal for Zoom in the mornings

159
00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:32.510
and then doing some running.

160
00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:35.160
I'm 75 miles into that 100 mile challenge

161
00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.513
we spoke about last time for Giveback Homes.

162
00:06:39.094 --> 00:06:42.540
The Giveback homies sent us some hats.

163
00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:43.970
And I've been swimming like once a week.

164
00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:45.280
I need to swim more.

165
00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:47.380
My SWOLF is suffering.

166
00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:48.820
What is SWOLF, by the way?

167
00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:50.210
I've never heard that before.

168
00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:51.910
You never heard of SWOLF stroke?

169
00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:55.820
It's like a stroke score that like comes up on my Garmin.

170
00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:57.210
Oh, I have no idea.

171
00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:58.650
I don't pay attention to that stuff.

172
00:06:58.650 --> 00:06:59.483
No?
No.

173
00:06:59.483 --> 00:07:02.980
I'm a purist naturalist when it comes to swimming.

174
00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:04.710
You swim naked?

175
00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:07.680
I have. Not recently.

176
00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.460
But yeah, with all the kind of data metrics on swimming,

177
00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:13.370
I mean, I wear a Garmin

178
00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:15.850
so that I can keep track of my yardage

179
00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:18.740
and time and stuff like that, but I don't look at cadence

180
00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:20.790
or any of the other metrics that tracks--

181
00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:22.050
Doesn't bother you, it doesn't matter,

182
00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:24.670
your stroke rate doesn't even register for you?

183
00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:26.220
I've just been doing it so long

184
00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:29.370
that I don't rely upon that for anything meaningful.

185
00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:30.910
Just feel.
Yeah.

186
00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:31.770
I like it.

187
00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:33.070
What does that stand for even?

188
00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:35.256
You know what, I thought you would know.

189
00:07:35.256 --> 00:07:36.710
No, I don't know.
SWOLF--

190
00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:39.610
Maybe this is why swimmers are so much faster now

191
00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:41.110
than they were when I was coming up.

192
00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:43.200
It's a combination of stroke count

193
00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.550
and time taken in the water,

194
00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:47.350
and is often used as a measure for your swimming efficiency.

195
00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:51.760
Ah, so how much forward propulsion

196
00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:54.415
are you getting per stroke kind of--

197
00:07:54.415 --> 00:07:55.400
Yeah.
Yeah, I get it.

198
00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:56.640
That's helpful.

199
00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:58.140
Maybe I should look at that.

200
00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:59.660
You seem like a guy that would,

201
00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:01.804
you'd probably have a really good SWOLF.

202
00:08:01.804 --> 00:08:03.766
That would mean that I need to be swimming.

203
00:08:03.766 --> 00:08:04.796
I'm not right now.

204
00:08:04.796 --> 00:08:05.740
Yeah, how are you? What's going on?

205
00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:07.130
What's going on with you?

206
00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:08.780
Oh man, Adam.

207
00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:10.460
Yeah, talk to me, bro.

208
00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:12.757
The back is not good.
What's going on?

209
00:08:12.757 --> 00:08:13.650
Which is one of the reason why we're not gonna do

210
00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:15.663
a check in with Chris Hauth today.

211
00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:19.260
It's really painful right now.

212
00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:21.400
If I try to do anything physical,

213
00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:24.380
the next day I have a real struggle.

214
00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:28.520
So first of all, we shared a couple weeks ago about this,

215
00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:31.310
I received, I was just inundated with emails

216
00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:34.020
and DMs from people so I appreciate everybody's

217
00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.730
sort of sympathy and concern.

218
00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:38.310
That means a lot.

219
00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:40.460
It also came with a lot of recommendations,

220
00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:43.670
a lot of, "Check out this guy.

221
00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:44.940
Here's my protocol.

222
00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:46.830
Here's how I solved my back problem."

223
00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:47.890
And that's great.

224
00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:50.450
I have to sift through it and figure out

225
00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:52.380
what else I can or should be doing

226
00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:56.170
beyond the protocol that I'm currently engaged in

227
00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:59.170
'cause I'm not experiencing any kind of relief.

228
00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:01.490
If I do nothing, then it's a little bit better.

229
00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:04.130
But I was in D.C. the other weekend

230
00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:07.010
visiting my parents and I had a little speaking gig,

231
00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:10.040
I couldn't resist the temptation of going out for a jog.

232
00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:12.470
D.C. is such a great city to run in.

233
00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:13.920
The weather was perfect.

234
00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.210
So I thought, "All right, I'm just gonna do

235
00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:19.070
the slowest run ever," like almost a walk,

236
00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:21.210
like a really gentle jog

237
00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:24.680
just so I can cruise around the mall, which I did,

238
00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:26.260
and I was very grateful to be able to do.

239
00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:27.750
And I felt okay while I was doing it,

240
00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:29.997
I didn't do anything hard, I didn't push myself at all.

241
00:09:29.997 --> 00:09:32.810
And the next day, I was like, oh, I couldn't sit up in bed.

242
00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:33.643
Really?
Yeah.

243
00:09:33.643 --> 00:09:35.270
And I haven't been back to the pool.

244
00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:39.770
So I'm pretty much benched right now and it's challenging.

245
00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:42.330
It's much easier for me to train.

246
00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:45.170
The physical exertion is so tied to my mental health

247
00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:47.570
that I'm not happy about it,

248
00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:49.130
but this is where I'm at right now

249
00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:52.363
until I figure out a path forward that is,

250
00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:55.179
got some daylight at the end of it.

251
00:09:55.179 --> 00:09:57.930
Is it a herniated disc type situation?

252
00:09:57.930 --> 00:09:59.140
No, it's not.

253
00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:03.130
It's spondy, my L4 protrudes about a centimeter

254
00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:08.130
towards my gut, like forward, it's out of alignment,

255
00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:10.760
and it puts pressure on the nerves

256
00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:12.850
that have created a lot of foot pain

257
00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:14.290
and some numbness in my left foot

258
00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:15.930
and just general discomfort.

259
00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:19.700
And I think the muscles that surround my spine

260
00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:24.470
are working so hard to keep it all intact

261
00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:27.873
that they just get really tense.

262
00:10:28.894 --> 00:10:30.140
