00:52:57.990 --> 00:52:59.310
during Ironman week

1291
00:52:59.310 --> 00:53:02.581
and all we do is go freediving and interview freedivers?

1292
00:53:02.581 --> 00:53:04.120
(laughs)

1293
00:53:04.120 --> 00:53:06.538
That seems like a bad use of budget.

1294
00:53:06.538 --> 00:53:07.660
I know, yeah.

1295
00:53:07.660 --> 00:53:11.200
Anyway. All right, switching gears here.

1296
00:53:11.200 --> 00:53:13.360
This week, again, it's the 10th anniversary

1297
00:53:13.360 --> 00:53:14.940
of "Finding Ultra", my first book.

1298
00:53:14.940 --> 00:53:19.070
This has given me a moment to kind of reflect

1299
00:53:19.070 --> 00:53:21.840
on this journey, which has been a really cool journey.

1300
00:53:21.840 --> 00:53:23.540
That book really set in motion

1301
00:53:23.540 --> 00:53:25.610
all of the things that I get to do today

1302
00:53:25.610 --> 00:53:28.690
and I'm very grateful for the experience

1303
00:53:28.690 --> 00:53:31.810
of writing that book and sharing that story with the world.

1304
00:53:31.810 --> 00:53:35.250
And yeah, I just kind of wanted to reflect back on that

1305
00:53:35.250 --> 00:53:36.280
and celebrate it.

1306
00:53:36.280 --> 00:53:39.700
I posted a tweet thread on Sunday

1307
00:53:39.700 --> 00:53:42.360
with a few things that I've learned along the way

1308
00:53:42.360 --> 00:53:44.900
as a result of writing this book.

1309
00:53:44.900 --> 00:53:46.680
Not the least of which is,

1310
00:53:46.680 --> 00:53:49.390
often when I get interviewed or I see press about myself,

1311
00:53:49.390 --> 00:53:51.380
it says, "New York Times' best-selling author,"

1312
00:53:51.380 --> 00:53:52.213
and I always laugh.

1313
00:53:52.213 --> 00:53:53.730
It's like this book was not

1314
00:53:53.730 --> 00:53:55.410
a New York Times best-selling book.

1315
00:53:55.410 --> 00:53:57.680
It never made the New York Times.

1316
00:53:57.680 --> 00:53:59.250
It didn't make any of those list.

1317
00:53:59.250 --> 00:54:01.090
Like it came out, it did fine

1318
00:54:02.930 --> 00:54:04.750
and it found a little bit of an audience.

1319
00:54:04.750 --> 00:54:08.480
And from there, it's just been a very slow build.

1320
00:54:08.480 --> 00:54:10.480
Obviously, the podcast has been helpful

1321
00:54:10.480 --> 00:54:13.780
in it reaching more people.

1322
00:54:13.780 --> 00:54:17.060
But just this kind of steady uptick year-after-year

1323
00:54:17.060 --> 00:54:19.730
of people finding the people and enjoying it and sharing it,

1324
00:54:19.730 --> 00:54:22.160
which is kind of like this podcast,

1325
00:54:22.160 --> 00:54:24.150
and it's really emblematic

1326
00:54:24.150 --> 00:54:26.650
of like every success that I've had.

1327
00:54:26.650 --> 00:54:28.890
Like I haven't had big viral moments.

1328
00:54:28.890 --> 00:54:29.723
Like I said in the tweet thread,

1329
00:54:29.723 --> 00:54:32.210
I haven't been on "Ellen" or "The Today Show"

1330
00:54:32.210 --> 00:54:35.820
or done a lot of mainstream stuff.

1331
00:54:35.820 --> 00:54:38.240
I mean, maybe the most mainstream thing

1332
00:54:38.240 --> 00:54:40.650
was the New York Times article that you wrote about me,

1333
00:54:40.650 --> 00:54:42.200
which I'm very grateful for.

1334
00:54:42.200 --> 00:54:44.460
That was many, many years after this book came out.

1335
00:54:44.460 --> 00:54:47.500
But everything that I've been able to achieve

1336
00:54:47.500 --> 00:54:49.470
has been a really slow burn.

1337
00:54:49.470 --> 00:54:53.370
And so it was kind of a meditation on leaning into that.

1338
00:54:53.370 --> 00:54:54.203
On the long game.

1339
00:54:54.203 --> 00:54:55.650
Yeah, the long, it's all about,

1340
00:54:55.650 --> 00:54:59.030
and as an ultra-endurance athlete, like it's fitting.

1341
00:54:59.030 --> 00:55:01.250
Yeah, I love that tweet thread.

1342
00:55:01.250 --> 00:55:02.850
I thought it was cool.

1343
00:55:02.850 --> 00:55:04.980
You say that the book has sold better each year

1344
00:55:04.980 --> 00:55:06.590
since it was published.

1345
00:55:06.590 --> 00:55:09.300
Yeah, I don't know if that's entirely accurate.

1346
00:55:09.300 --> 00:55:12.610
I know it continues to sell, and I would have to go back

1347
00:55:12.610 --> 00:55:14.470
and look at the specific numbers.

1348
00:55:14.470 --> 00:55:18.180
I'm sure there's been ebbs and flows with that.

1349
00:55:18.180 --> 00:55:20.520
But yeah, I mean, I just know

1350
00:55:20.520 --> 00:55:23.750
from getting the quarterly statements or whatever

1351
00:55:23.750 --> 00:55:26.590
that it continues to sell as well as it ever has.

1352
00:55:26.590 --> 00:55:29.100
I mean, obviously the first week it comes out

1353
00:55:29.100 --> 00:55:30.230
like you're gonna sell a lot

1354
00:55:30.230 --> 00:55:33.400
'cause it includes those pre-order numbers,

1355
00:55:33.400 --> 00:55:35.190
and then after that there's a huge dip.

1356
00:55:35.190 --> 00:55:36.740
And then from there forward,

1357
00:55:36.740 --> 00:55:39.380
it's just been kind of a solid growth curve.

1358
00:55:39.380 --> 00:55:42.320
Well, do you mind if I ask some probing questions?

1359
00:55:42.320 --> 00:55:44.430
Sure.
On the record?

1360
00:55:44.430 --> 00:55:46.140
There's no off the record here.

1361
00:55:46.140 --> 00:55:47.470
Never.

1362
00:55:47.470 --> 00:55:49.040
Is that what you tell all your sources?

1363
00:55:49.040 --> 00:55:50.397
No, what I tell them is,

1364
00:55:50.397 --> 00:55:53.990
"Anything you want off the record, just let me know."

1365
00:55:53.990 --> 00:55:55.110
But not you, Rich.

1366
00:55:55.110 --> 00:55:57.033
No. Okay, I'm game.

1367
00:55:58.370 --> 00:56:00.580
So tell us a little bit about

1368
00:56:00.580 --> 00:56:01.760
like where you were in your life

1369
00:56:01.760 --> 00:56:02.810
when you decided to write the book,

1370
00:56:02.810 --> 00:56:05.100
'cause you've already done these amazing feats,

1371
00:56:05.100 --> 00:56:07.390
you're already kind of, you're on the path,

1372
00:56:07.390 --> 00:56:11.200
you're sober, you're an endurance athlete again,

1373
00:56:11.200 --> 00:56:14.580
you're fit, so like what, where were you,

1374
00:56:14.580 --> 00:56:18.100
how many years after EPIC5, how did all come together?

1375
00:56:18.100 --> 00:56:22.940
It came together fairly shortly after EPIC5.

1376
00:56:22.940 --> 00:56:27.940
I had come back from that and was trying to figure out

1377
00:56:27.980 --> 00:56:29.963
what I was gonna do next.

1378
00:56:31.530 --> 00:56:32.680
And it was a challenging time.

1379
00:56:32.680 --> 00:56:35.700
Like I was still a practicing lawyer at that time

1380
00:56:35.700 --> 00:56:38.090
but I had lost so much interest in pursuing that

1381
00:56:38.090 --> 00:56:41.040
that I wasn't really making very much money doing that

1382
00:56:41.040 --> 00:56:44.540
and was really struggling to pay the bills

1383
00:56:44.540 --> 00:56:47.613
and to figure out how to provide for my family.

1384
00:56:48.470 --> 00:56:52.810
And the way it came together is kind of a cool story.

1385
00:56:52.810 --> 00:56:54.960
There had been a little blurb about me

1386
00:56:54.960 --> 00:56:57.640
in the Stanford alumni magazine

1387
00:56:57.640 --> 00:56:59.390
about how I had done these races

1388
00:56:59.390 --> 00:57:04.130
and it mentioned that I had been sober for a while.

1389
00:57:04.130 --> 00:57:06.190
And in the wake of that article coming out,

1390
00:57:06.190 --> 00:57:11.190
I got a email from a guy who had been a swimmer at Stanford,

1391
00:57:12.090 --> 00:57:13.020
who was somebody I didn't know,

1392
00:57:13.020 --> 00:57:15.870
he was several years older than me.

1393
00:57:15.870 --> 00:57:19.790
And he just said, "Listen, I saw that article

1394
00:57:19.790 --> 00:57:21.790
in the alumni magazine.

1395
00:57:21.790 --> 00:57:25.780
I'm newly sober and it would be good

1396
00:57:25.780 --> 00:57:27.260
to be able to talk to somebody.

1397
00:57:27.260 --> 00:57:28.800
Could we like talk on the phone?"

1398
00:57:28.800 --> 00:57:32.360
And I just called him up, or he called me.

1399
00:57:32.360 --> 00:57:35.190
And this guy had recently gotten out of treatment,

1400
00:57:35.190 --> 00:57:37.520
he's CEO of a large company,

1401
00:57:37.520 --> 00:57:41.330
nobody on his, in his company knew

1402
00:57:41.330 --> 00:57:42.610
what was going on with him

1403
00:57:42.610 --> 00:57:45.090
and he was trying to navigate like early sobriety

1404
00:57:45.090 --> 00:57:47.630
and all of the kind of challenges of that.

1405
00:57:47.630 --> 00:57:51.360
And I just was there to be of service to this guy

1406
00:57:51.360 --> 00:57:54.787
and share whatever I could to help him and that was it.

1407
00:57:54.787 --> 00:57:56.730
And we had a number of calls,

1408
00:57:56.730 --> 00:57:59.610
talked every couple of weeks for a period of time.

1409
00:57:59.610 --> 00:58:02.407
And at some point, he said to me like,

1410
00:58:02.407 --> 00:58:04.950
"You have a pretty cool story.

1411
00:58:04.950 --> 00:58:07.480
Like have you ever thought about writing a book?"

1412
00:58:07.480 --> 00:58:11.103
And I hadn't seriously ever thought of writing a book.

1413
00:58:12.280 --> 00:58:13.785
I had thought like,

1414
00:58:13.785 --> 00:58:15.570
"Maybe that would be a cool thing to do,"

1415
00:58:15.570 --> 00:58:17.820
but it wasn't like I had ever taken any active steps

1416
00:58:17.820 --> 00:58:20.830
towards pursuing that in any meaningful way.

1417
00:58:20.830 --> 00:58:22.830
He was like, "Oh, I know this book agent.

1418
00:58:22.830 --> 00:58:25.530
I should introduce you," and he did.

1419
00:58:25.530 --> 00:58:28.100
And that led to me being on the phone with Carole Bidnick,

1420
00:58:28.100 --> 00:58:30.320
who became my agent at the time.

1421
00:58:30.320 --> 00:58:32.217
And I told her the story, and she said,

1422
00:58:32.217 --> 00:58:35.690
"Well, books like this are hard.

1423
00:58:35.690 --> 00:58:37.750
You really have to thread the needle.

1424
00:58:37.750 --> 00:58:40.020
I'd be happy to read a proposal

1425
00:58:40.020 --> 00:58:42.920
and happy to help kinda babysit you through

1426
00:58:42.920 --> 00:58:45.710
how to put a proposal together if you're interested.

1427
00:58:45.710 --> 00:58:49.080
I can't make any promises," and that was kinda that.

1428
00:58:49.080 --> 00:58:53.130
But it was a crack in the, it was like a little crack,

1429
00:58:53.130 --> 00:58:54.810
the door cracked open a little bit.

1430
00:58:54.810 --> 00:58:56.320
And I've just learned over the years

1431
00:58:56.320 --> 00:58:57.870
when you have that little opportunity,

1432
00:58:57.870 --> 00:58:59.460
like those don't come around that often.

1433
00:58:59.460 --> 00:59:01.930
So I was like, this is something I'm gonna take

1434
00:59:01.930 --> 00:59:02.940
really seriously.

1435
00:59:02.940 --> 00:59:05.740
And I worked really hard for like three or four months

1436
00:59:05.740 --> 00:59:08.170
on a proposal, which entails,

1437
00:59:08.170 --> 00:59:10.400
it's like writing a business plan for a book, as you know,

1438
00:59:10.400 --> 00:59:11.762
'cause you've done it.

1439
00:59:11.762 --> 00:59:14.240
Like you have to create a marketing plan

1440
00:59:14.240 --> 00:59:15.770
and you have to synopsize the book

1441
00:59:15.770 --> 00:59:18.460
and you have to write a couple chapters of it

1442
00:59:18.460 --> 00:59:19.980
and you gotta outline the whole thing,

1443
00:59:19.980 --> 00:59:23.660
like you gotta kinda spell out the entire project

1444
00:59:23.660 --> 00:59:25.000
and also make the case

1445
00:59:25.000 --> 00:59:28.950
for why it's viable in the marketplace.

1446
00:59:28.950 --> 00:59:31.130
And I'd never done anything that before.

1447
00:59:31.130 --> 00:59:32.560
But I worked really, really hard on it

1448
00:59:32.560 --> 00:59:33.890
'cause I knew like this is a shot

1449
00:59:33.890 --> 00:59:35.950
that doesn't come around that often.

1450
00:59:35.950 --> 00:59:38.890
And when it was done, send it to her,

1451
00:59:38.890 --> 00:59:40.870
and she said, "This is good.

1452
00:59:40.870 --> 00:59:42.780
But again, like this is really hard

1453
00:59:42.780 --> 00:59:47.780
and I've got a couple publishing potential buyers here

1454
00:59:48.017 --> 00:59:49.870
who I think might be interested in this,

1455
00:59:49.870 --> 00:59:52.580
but like we'll see," like she was very tempered

1456
00:59:52.580 --> 00:59:53.530
in the whole thing.

1457
00:59:54.660 --> 00:59:56.820
But she said, "I'm gonna slip it to a few people

1458
00:59:56.820 --> 00:59:58.060
and I'll be in touch."

1459
00:59:58.060 --> 01:00:00.460
And literally, like within a day,

1460
01:00:00.460 --> 01:00:04.980
I'd got an interest from Random House, Rick Horgan,

1461
01:00:04.980 --> 01:00:08.510
who's editor there, who's a big editor at Crown

1462
01:00:08.510 --> 01:00:11.870
at the time, was interested.

1463
01:00:11.870 --> 01:00:14.140
And Carole's like, "Let's do a call."

1464
01:00:14.140 --> 01:00:16.310
And I still remember the call.

1465
01:00:16.310 --> 01:00:20.220
I was like at the, at this, at the Calabasas Commons,

1466
01:00:20.220 --> 01:00:21.550
which just like this outdoor mall,

1467
01:00:21.550 --> 01:00:23.490
like walking around when this call was scheduled

1468
01:00:23.490 --> 01:00:26.050
'cause I didn't have an office and I couldn't work at home.

1469
01:00:26.050 --> 01:00:27.910
We had young kids and all that kinda stuff.

1470
01:00:27.910 --> 01:00:30.940
And I would like go to the Barnes &amp; Noble,

1471
01:00:30.940 --> 01:00:33.247
which I had free Wi-Fi at the time, to work.

1472
01:00:33.247 --> 01:00:34.660
And so to do this call, where it was quiet,

1473
01:00:34.660 --> 01:00:36.650
I remember like going behind some bushes

1474
01:00:36.650 --> 01:00:39.320
to like do this pitch call.

1475
01:00:39.320 --> 01:00:43.460
And he just said, "Tell me about this project in the book,"

1476
01:00:43.460 --> 01:00:45.410
and I just laid it on thick

1477
01:00:45.410 --> 01:00:48.080
and like threw down the best case

1478
01:00:48.080 --> 01:00:49.880
that I could make on the phone.

1479
01:00:49.880 --> 01:00:52.210
And within 72 hours, we had a deal

1480
01:00:52.210 --> 01:00:53.450
that he took it off the table

1481
01:00:53.450 --> 01:00:55.920
and like, we were like off to the races.

1482
01:00:55.920 --> 01:00:58.493
And Carole was like, "That never happens.

1483
01:00:59.660 --> 01:01:00.560
You don't understand,

1484
01:01:00.560 --> 01:01:02.810
like this is always a much more protracted,

1485
01:01:02.810 --> 01:01:04.890
challenging situation."

1486
01:01:04.890 --> 01:01:08.170
So it was unbelievable, like I was,

1487
01:01:08.170 --> 01:01:09.730
I couldn't believe that this was happening.

1488
01:01:09.730 --> 01:01:14.730
And so there was another kind of crack, door cracking open.

1489
01:01:15.770 --> 01:01:16.690
Just 'cause you wrote a book,

1490
01:01:16.690 --> 01:01:19.010
doesn't mean it's gonna be good or successful.

1491
01:01:19.010 --> 01:01:22.060
So I really busted my ass for like a year

1492
01:01:22.060 --> 01:01:24.330
to put this book out and make--

1493
01:01:24.330 --> 01:01:27.740
Did you separate yourself from your law practice entirely

1494
01:01:27.740 --> 01:01:28.573
during that year?

1495
01:01:28.573 --> 01:01:29.850
No, I was straddling both.

1496
01:01:29.850 --> 01:01:34.370
Because I got like an okay advance for a first time writer.

1497
01:01:34.370 --> 01:01:37.930
But as you know, you see like, "Oh, it's this much money,"

1498
01:01:37.930 --> 01:01:42.320
but it's like, okay, you get 25% on signing

1499
01:01:42.320 --> 01:01:45.300
and then 50% when you deliver the manuscript

1500
01:01:45.300 --> 01:01:48.000
and then the other like whatever percent

1501
01:01:48.000 --> 01:01:48.910
when the book comes out.

1502
01:01:48.910 --> 01:01:51.990
So this is spread out over a long period of time.

1503
01:01:51.990 --> 01:01:54.340
And when you take out taxes and agency commission,

1504
01:01:54.340 --> 01:01:57.410
like it was, I was incredibly grateful for it.

1505
01:01:57.410 --> 01:02:00.640
It was huge, but it wasn't a situation

1506
01:02:00.640 --> 01:02:03.590
that was gonna be able to sustain my family

1507
01:02:03.590 --> 01:02:05.210
over a period of a year and a half.

1508
01:02:05.210 --> 01:02:07.010
So I had to supplement my income

1509
01:02:07.010 --> 01:02:09.200
with still practicing law at the time.

1510
01:02:09.200 --> 01:02:11.340
I didn't pull the plug on practicing law

1511
01:02:11.340 --> 01:02:14.790
until the book came out.

1512
01:02:14.790 --> 01:02:17.650
And then I was like, "I'm not renewing my bar membership."

1513
01:02:17.650 --> 01:02:18.483
Fantastic.

1514
01:02:18.483 --> 01:02:21.120
So after the book came out--

1515
01:02:21.120 --> 01:02:22.930
Yeah, then I was like, "I'm done."

1516
01:02:22.930 --> 01:02:26.120
And that was scary too, because you would think,

1517
01:02:26.120 --> 01:02:27.980
like when the book comes out,

1518
01:02:27.980 --> 01:02:30.350
oh, all these opportunities happen and like you,

1519
01:02:30.350 --> 01:02:33.800
you got to be this guy and go tour with the book,

1520
01:02:33.800 --> 01:02:36.040
and it was like, yeah, I did a little bit of,

1521
01:02:36.040 --> 01:02:38.110
I did a couple of appearances.

1522
01:02:38.110 --> 01:02:39.360
A few people came to them.

1523
01:02:39.360 --> 01:02:40.930
It wasn't any big deal.

1524
01:02:40.930 --> 01:02:43.410
And then it was like crickets.

1525
01:02:43.410 --> 01:02:45.000
Right.

1526
01:02:45.000 --> 01:02:46.520
And part, one of the lines in the book

1527
01:02:46.520 --> 01:02:48.290
is when your heart is true,

1528
01:02:48.290 --> 01:02:50.130
the universe will conspire to support you.

1529
01:02:50.130 --> 01:02:51.840
And I was like, "My heart's fucking true,

1530
01:02:51.840 --> 01:02:55.010
like the universe will conspire to support me.

1531
01:02:55.010 --> 01:02:56.290
I believe that."

1532
01:02:56.290 --> 01:02:59.120
What I realized is that it wasn't gonna be on my timeline.

1533
01:02:59.120 --> 01:03:00.683
No, not on your schedule.

1534
01:03:00.683 --> 01:03:02.450
And I had to suffer for quite a long time

1535
01:03:02.450 --> 01:03:04.230
before things started to turn around

1536
01:03:04.230 --> 01:03:07.000
and I was able to like create a vocation

1537
01:03:07.000 --> 01:03:09.180
out of what the book began.

1538
01:03:09.180 --> 01:03:11.830
So the book comes out, you cancel your bar membership,

1539
01:03:11.830 --> 01:03:14.020
so what are you doing like in that time?

1540
01:03:14.020 --> 01:03:16.010
Because even if it was selling well,

1541
01:03:16.010 --> 01:03:17.160
which you said it was doing okay,

1542
01:03:17.160 --> 01:03:18.480
but you didn't sound like you're gonna be

1543
01:03:18.480 --> 01:03:21.080
able to live off of that.

1544
01:03:21.080 --> 01:03:23.820
I mean, it was dark, dude.

1545
01:03:23.820 --> 01:03:24.840
That was the period of time.

1546
01:03:24.840 --> 01:03:27.560
And I've spoken about this many, many times before

1547
01:03:27.560 --> 01:03:29.940
so we don't need to go into detail around it.

1548
01:03:29.940 --> 01:03:31.870
But we had cars repossessed.

1549
01:03:31.870 --> 01:03:34.630
I couldn't pay for our garbage removal

1550
01:03:34.630 --> 01:03:36.610
so our bins got taken away.

1551
01:03:36.610 --> 01:03:40.330
We were driving around in a completely beat up minivan

1552
01:03:40.330 --> 01:03:42.140
that had like 200,000 miles on it,

1553
01:03:42.140 --> 01:03:43.290
that smelled like garbage

1554
01:03:43.290 --> 01:03:45.180
'cause we had to put our garbage in the minivan

1555
01:03:45.180 --> 01:03:48.410
and then find an empty dumpster somewhere to dump them.

1556
01:03:48.410 --> 01:03:52.010
Like it was humiliating and emasculating.

1557
01:03:52.010 --> 01:03:56.410
But I'd get the occasional 500 bucks to go speak somewhere

1558
01:03:56.410 --> 01:04:00.416
or some little opportunity would come along.

1559
01:04:00.416 --> 01:04:01.249
(clears throat)

1560
01:04:01.249 --> 01:04:04.160
But it was dark and we were drowning.

1561
01:04:04.160 --> 01:04:06.260
And that's when the opportunity to go to Kauai

1562
01:04:06.260 --> 01:04:08.863
and work with this guy Chris Jaeb came along.

1563
01:04:09.742 --> 01:04:10.630
(clears throat)

1564
01:04:10.630 --> 01:04:13.700
He threw us a lifeline to help him figure out

1565
01:04:14.870 --> 01:04:19.810
how to make use of this amazing property that he owned

1566
01:04:19.810 --> 01:04:20.810
on the north shore of Kauai.

1567
01:04:20.810 --> 01:04:25.370
And we moved to the Hanalei area and lived in Yurt,

1568
01:04:25.370 --> 01:04:26.710
on his organic farm.

1569
01:04:26.710 --> 01:04:29.450
And I worked with him for three months,

1570
01:04:29.450 --> 01:04:31.070
trying to help him as best I could

1571
01:04:31.070 --> 01:04:34.410
and he paid us and that allowed us to like pay our bills.

1572
01:04:34.410 --> 01:04:37.210
And it was like, I'm so incredibly grateful for.

1573
01:04:37.210 --> 01:04:38.650
It's confusing and weird

1574
01:04:38.650 --> 01:04:41.410
in that like I don't know why he thought I could be the,

1575
01:04:41.410 --> 01:04:44.730
I was the right person to fill this strange role

1576
01:04:44.730 --> 01:04:45.790
that he wanted filled,

1577
01:04:45.790 --> 01:04:49.030
but I'm extremely grateful to him because he,

1578
01:04:49.030 --> 01:04:51.650
short of that, like I don't know what we would've done.

1579
01:04:51.650 --> 01:04:54.020
But it was, during that period of time,

1580
01:04:54.020 --> 01:04:57.380
which was the winter of 2012

1581
01:04:58.350 --> 01:05:00.840
that I started the podcast there

1582
01:05:01.970 --> 01:05:04.860
and that's kind of what led to everything that's followed.

1583
01:05:04.860 --> 01:05:06.310
Yeah, right. You started that there.

1584
01:05:06.310 --> 01:05:10.410
So it is related to this, to the book in that regard, right?

1585
01:05:10.410 --> 01:05:13.370
The book happens, you leave law,

1586
01:05:13.370 --> 01:05:16.880
and then things kind of tailspin

1587
01:05:16.880 --> 01:05:18.340
and you end up landing in Kauai,

1588
01:05:18.340 --> 01:05:21.150
softly in a beautiful place and then start this podcast.

1589
01:05:21.150 --> 01:05:22.130
Yeah.

1590
01:05:22.130 --> 01:05:26.040
And to be clear, like the podcast was a fun,

1591
01:05:26.040 --> 01:05:27.670
creative passion project.

1592
01:05:27.670 --> 01:05:31.070
It was never considered to be something

1593
01:05:31.070 --> 01:05:34.050
that would generate money

1594
01:05:34.050 --> 01:05:36.530
that would allow me to support my family.

1595
01:05:36.530 --> 01:05:40.870
And for a snapshot of what podcasting was like in 2012,

1596
01:05:40.870 --> 01:05:42.300
it's nothing like it is today.

1597
01:05:42.300 --> 01:05:45.710
Like it wasn't cool to start a podcast.

1598
01:05:45.710 --> 01:05:47.790
I didn't know anybody other than myself

1599
01:05:47.790 --> 01:05:49.330
that listened to podcasts.

1600
01:05:49.330 --> 01:05:51.450
It was difficult to download a podcast.

1601
01:05:51.450 --> 01:05:54.030
This was before the iPhone and streaming.

1602
01:05:54.030 --> 01:05:57.150
You had to download it on your desktop or your laptop

1603
01:05:57.150 --> 01:05:58.850
and then bounce it to an iPod

1604
01:05:58.850 --> 01:06:01.230
to create these like playlists.

1605
01:06:01.230 --> 01:06:02.671
It was a thing.

1606
01:06:02.671 --> 01:06:06.550
And at the same time, it was also a moment in time

1607
01:06:06.550 --> 01:06:10.530
where no, it wasn't like people were starting podcasts

1608
01:06:10.530 --> 01:06:11.363
all over the place.

1609
01:06:11.363 --> 01:06:13.500
So from the very first episode,

1610
01:06:13.500 --> 01:06:15.660
we were able to make an impression on iTunes

1611
01:06:15.660 --> 01:06:18.830
and solidify like a position in the charts

1612
01:06:18.830 --> 01:06:20.340
and the health space.

1613
01:06:20.340 --> 01:06:23.570
And that, I think, opened the door for us

1614
01:06:23.570 --> 01:06:26.280
to develop a listenership

1615
01:06:26.280 --> 01:06:28.550
in a way that would be much more difficult today.

1616
01:06:28.550 --> 01:06:30.253
Hmm, yeah.

1617
01:06:31.300 --> 01:06:35.280
I can relate to thinking, you get the book come out,

1618
01:06:35.280 --> 01:06:37.860
you think you made it to a certain level, and you have,

1619
01:06:37.860 --> 01:06:41.770
'cause it does establish kind of,

1620
01:06:41.770 --> 01:06:43.890
it is an establishing thing for you

1621
01:06:43.890 --> 01:06:45.700
because there is a perception around it,

1622
01:06:45.700 --> 01:06:48.230
especially if the book has either sold well

1623
01:06:48.230 --> 01:06:51.110
or been reviewed well, but even not,

1624
01:06:51.110 --> 01:06:53.870
just if it has some sort of anything around it.

1625
01:06:53.870 --> 01:06:55.000
Like in my case, the book,

1626
01:06:55.000 --> 01:06:57.520
my book didn't, "One Breath" didn't sell as well,

1627
01:06:57.520 --> 01:07:00.830
but it had really good reviews in some places.

1628
01:07:00.830 --> 01:07:03.570
And so then, but then afterwards

1629
01:07:04.657 --> 01:07:06.863
(laughs) you're trying to,

1630
01:07:07.974 --> 01:07:09.697
you have to then keep making a living, right?

1631
01:07:09.697 --> 01:07:12.010
And for some reason, you take your foot off the gas.

1632
01:07:12.010 --> 01:07:15.190
I did, I took my foot off the gas a little bit

1633
01:07:15.190 --> 01:07:20.190
because I wasn't like, I didn't wanna sell myself that hard.

1634
01:07:20.640 --> 01:07:23.420
I didn't really push the PR as hard as,

1635
01:07:23.420 --> 01:07:24.720
like if I had to do it again,

1636
01:07:24.720 --> 01:07:26.210
I think I would do things a lot differently

1637
01:07:26.210 --> 01:07:27.660
and I never would take my foot off the gas.

1638
01:07:27.660 --> 01:07:29.220
But you have to go through that to know that.

1639
01:07:29.220 --> 01:07:31.660
You have to be utterly shameless in that phase.

1640
01:07:31.660 --> 01:07:34.580
And that's not really in the wiring of most writers.

1641
01:07:34.580 --> 01:07:37.530
Like they write so they don't have to interact with people.

1642
01:07:38.450 --> 01:07:40.567
They write because they are ashamed.

1643
01:07:40.567 --> 01:07:41.870
(both laugh)

1644
01:07:41.870 --> 01:07:45.260
Well, like many artists, it's like, "I did the thing,

1645
01:07:45.260 --> 01:07:46.970
you go push it out into the world."

1646
01:07:46.970 --> 01:07:49.470
Like, "I already created my thing."

1647
01:07:49.470 --> 01:07:51.980
And sometimes there's an expectation

1648
01:07:51.980 --> 01:07:54.830
that the world is awaiting this great work.

1649
01:07:54.830 --> 01:07:57.150
Like nobody's waiting for your thing.

1650
01:07:57.150 --> 01:07:57.983
I didn't think that.

1651
01:07:57.983 --> 01:07:58.830
You gotta make it happen.

1652
01:07:58.830 --> 01:08:01.370
And I remember getting counsel from some people

1653
01:08:01.370 --> 01:08:04.410
who are like, "You have to do everything in your power

1654
01:08:04.410 --> 01:08:05.780
to make people care about this,"

1655
01:08:05.780 --> 01:08:07.833
and I treated that like my job,

1656
01:08:08.710 --> 01:08:11.100
did the best that I could with the small

1657
01:08:11.100 --> 01:08:14.423
sort of interest, public interest, that I had at the time.

1658
01:08:16.480 --> 01:08:18.777
But yeah, it kind of comes and goes.

1659
01:08:18.777 --> 01:08:21.840
And it's a weird dissociative experience

1660
01:08:21.840 --> 01:08:23.560
because on the one hand like I couldn't believe

1661
01:08:23.560 --> 01:08:25.820
I was being given the opportunity to write a book.

1662
01:08:25.820 --> 01:08:27.860
And then that book, you could go to Barnes &amp; Noble

1663
01:08:27.860 --> 01:08:30.010
and see it on the shelf, it's like mind-blowing.

1664
01:08:30.010 --> 01:08:31.360
Right. Yeah, that was cool.

1665
01:08:31.360 --> 01:08:32.210
It's like I can't even believe.

1666
01:08:32.210 --> 01:08:33.340
This is like so cool.

1667
01:08:33.340 --> 01:08:36.090
Like travel to a different city and go into a bookstore

1668
01:08:36.090 --> 01:08:38.760
and your book is there, like wild, right?

1669
01:08:38.760 --> 01:08:40.750
And the fact that 10 people

1670
01:08:40.750 --> 01:08:43.430
would come to like hear me talk about it,

1671
01:08:43.430 --> 01:08:46.225
like who are these people, like that's crazy.

1672
01:08:46.225 --> 01:08:50.710
So that was all gravy and amazing.

1673
01:08:50.710 --> 01:08:52.970
But I think there's a public perception

1674
01:08:52.970 --> 01:08:54.270
that if you're a published author

1675
01:08:54.270 --> 01:08:55.680
and your book is in a bookstore

1676
01:08:55.680 --> 01:08:57.920
that like you don't have to worry about paying your bills

1677
01:08:57.920 --> 01:09:00.250
and I can tell you that that's certainly not the case.

1678
01:09:00.250 --> 01:09:01.400
The opposite is true.

1679
01:09:03.400 --> 01:09:06.410
The writing style itself, like obviously as a lawyer,

1680
01:09:06.410 --> 01:09:08.320
people know lawyers write a lot,

1681
01:09:08.320 --> 01:09:10.560
but they don't write in this particular way.

1682
01:09:10.560 --> 01:09:13.540
They write, a lot of lawyers write in stilted,

1683
01:09:13.540 --> 01:09:16.000
highly specific ways.

1684
01:09:16.000 --> 01:09:18.430
You were able to, in your first crack,

1685
01:09:18.430 --> 01:09:20.870
like I write all the time so like,

1686
01:09:20.870 --> 01:09:23.590
it's part of my musculature, you do now as well,

1687
01:09:23.590 --> 01:09:26.310
but in, often for the podcast,

1688
01:09:26.310 --> 01:09:29.700
but for this book, you weren't coming

1689
01:09:29.700 --> 01:09:32.770
from like writing every day kind of background.

1690
01:09:32.770 --> 01:09:33.603
Well, yes and no.

1691
01:09:33.603 --> 01:09:36.290
I mean, first of all, have you read my demand letters

1692
01:09:36.290 --> 01:09:38.440
from my lawyer days?

1693
01:09:38.440 --> 01:09:40.153
I would not call them stilted.

1694
01:09:41.133 --> 01:09:42.277
Very direct.

1695
01:09:42.277 --> 01:09:43.390
Writing a great, snarky demand letter

1696
01:09:43.390 --> 01:09:45.380
is a art form in and of itself.

1697
01:09:45.380 --> 01:09:47.110
You should do a collection.

1698
01:09:47.110 --> 01:09:50.560
Yeah, there's some great ones like, you know.

1699
01:09:50.560 --> 01:09:53.600
I am in receipt of your, you know.

1700
01:09:53.600 --> 01:09:56.800
I assure you that my client, you know, and whatever.

1701
01:09:56.800 --> 01:09:59.810
No, I was a creative writer long before I was a lawyer.

1702
01:09:59.810 --> 01:10:03.390
Like I did a lot of writing in college, I always loved it.

1703
01:10:03.390 --> 01:10:06.590
And law school and being a practicing lawyer

1704
01:10:06.590 --> 01:10:10.230
I think only enhances one's writing skill

1705
01:10:10.230 --> 01:10:12.830
because it lends structure to it.

1706
01:10:12.830 --> 01:10:15.820
It provides like a framework for making an argument

1707
01:10:15.820 --> 01:10:17.990
and supporting that argument

1708
01:10:17.990 --> 01:10:20.700
as long as you hold on to whatever

1709
01:10:20.700 --> 01:10:23.170
creative flare that you have.

1710
01:10:23.170 --> 01:10:24.980
But just the practice of always being,

1711
01:10:24.980 --> 01:10:26.070
writing when you're a lawyer

1712
01:10:26.070 --> 01:10:28.460
I think keeps that muscle strong.

1713
01:10:28.460 --> 01:10:32.090
But I think a lot, the sensibility of the book

1714
01:10:33.120 --> 01:10:36.530
has less to do with writing skill

1715
01:10:36.530 --> 01:10:41.100
and more about like a willingness to be vulnerable

1716
01:10:41.100 --> 01:10:43.650
and tell stories that most people

1717
01:10:43.650 --> 01:10:46.678
would maybe be too uncomfortable to show.

1718
01:10:46.678 --> 01:10:47.517
Right, but you tell 'em as if you're,

1719
01:10:47.517 --> 01:10:50.010
you're writing as you talk, which is what you should do

1720
01:10:50.010 --> 01:10:52.430
and so it is a very natural voice in the book.

1721
01:10:52.430 --> 01:10:54.990
Yeah. I mean, I don't know about you when you're writing,

1722
01:10:54.990 --> 01:10:57.890
but for me, the approach with this book is,

1723
01:10:57.890 --> 01:11:02.890
was to overwrite it, like just write everything

1724
01:11:02.960 --> 01:11:06.280
and then figure out what the salient stories are

1725
01:11:06.280 --> 01:11:10.420
and call out everything that isn't moving the story forward.

1726
01:11:10.420 --> 01:11:12.880
And I work with it, Rick Horgan was my editor,

1727
01:11:12.880 --> 01:11:14.870
but I also work with another editor

1728
01:11:14.870 --> 01:11:15.940
'cause I think you need that,

1729
01:11:15.940 --> 01:11:17.800
that sounding board or that feedback

1730
01:11:17.800 --> 01:11:19.750
because you're too in the middle of it,

1731
01:11:19.750 --> 01:11:21.720
like it's your story.

1732
01:11:21.720 --> 01:11:25.500
So having perspective is important as well.

1733
01:11:25.500 --> 01:11:30.500
But I think most memoirs suffer from lack of editing,

1734
01:11:30.840 --> 01:11:32.160
like there's too much in there.

1735
01:11:32.160 --> 01:11:33.390
It's not like,

1736
01:11:33.390 --> 01:11:36.020
if you're telling your, quote, unquote, "life story",

1737
01:11:36.020 --> 01:11:37.640
you're not supposed to say,

1738
01:11:37.640 --> 01:11:40.020
tell every story that ever happened to you

1739
01:11:40.020 --> 01:11:40.870
that's interesting.

1740
01:11:40.870 --> 01:11:43.370
Like what is propelling the story forward,

1741
01:11:43.370 --> 01:11:45.160
that pivots on the themes

1742
01:11:45.160 --> 01:11:49.830
that you are finding most important and telling that story?

1743
01:11:49.830 --> 01:11:52.470
Well, let's talk about the craft of memoir.

1744
01:11:52.470 --> 01:11:55.853
What do you think makes a good or great memoir?

1745
01:11:57.459 --> 01:11:58.292
That's a good question.

1746
01:11:58.292 --> 01:12:00.750
I wanna throw that to you as well,

1747
01:12:00.750 --> 01:12:03.140
as somebody who's written memoirs.

1748
01:12:03.140 --> 01:12:08.140
I mean, for me, my book kinda falls

1749
01:12:09.090 --> 01:12:10.970
into the sports memoir category.

1750
01:12:10.970 --> 01:12:11.803
Right.

1751
01:12:11.803 --> 01:12:15.240
And I think most sports memoirs suck.

1752
01:12:15.240 --> 01:12:16.570
They're terrible.

1753
01:12:16.570 --> 01:12:18.950
Because they're vainglorious.

1754
01:12:18.950 --> 01:12:23.230
Like they're generally written by a ghostwriter

1755
01:12:23.230 --> 01:12:27.780
in the twilight of a certain athlete's career

1756
01:12:27.780 --> 01:12:30.240
as an exercise in extending

1757
01:12:31.230 --> 01:12:35.130
that particular athlete's brand viability.

1758
01:12:35.130 --> 01:12:39.360
And so they tend to focus on all the great stuff

1759
01:12:39.360 --> 01:12:42.670
that they did, but they rarely paint a full picture

1760
01:12:42.670 --> 01:12:44.090
of that person's life.

1761
01:12:44.090 --> 01:12:49.090
Like they don't typically have the level of candor

1762
01:12:49.560 --> 01:12:54.140
and honesty and vulnerability that I think is critical

1763
01:12:54.140 --> 01:12:55.760
to connecting with a readership.

1764
01:12:55.760 --> 01:12:56.930
I mean, there's exceptions to that.

1765
01:12:56.930 --> 01:12:59.950
I think Andre Agassi's memoir is exceptional

1766
01:12:59.950 --> 01:13:01.600
for that reason.

1767
01:13:01.600 --> 01:13:06.600
So for me, I didn't wanna be influenced by that genre

1768
01:13:07.460 --> 01:13:09.960
so I didn't go out and read a bunch of memoirs

1769
01:13:09.960 --> 01:13:13.860
and try to deconstruct what would make for a good memoir.

1770
01:13:13.860 --> 01:13:16.200
I just tried to tell my story as openly

1771
01:13:16.200 --> 01:13:18.530
and as honestly as I could.

1772
01:13:18.530 --> 01:13:21.080
And the framework that I used for that

1773
01:13:21.080 --> 01:13:24.110
is sort of the what happened,

1774
01:13:24.110 --> 01:13:26.770
what it was like, what happened, and what it's like now,

1775
01:13:26.770 --> 01:13:29.307
which is like the 12-step format of sharing your story.

1776
01:13:29.307 --> 01:13:34.070
And somebody sat in tens of thousands of 12-step meetings

1777
01:13:34.070 --> 01:13:37.600
and listened to people share their stories

1778
01:13:37.600 --> 01:13:39.730
and I've had the opportunity to share my own

1779
01:13:39.730 --> 01:13:41.610
many, many times.

1780
01:13:41.610 --> 01:13:44.237
That was really more of where my head was at like,

1781
01:13:44.237 --> 01:13:49.060
"How can I tell my story in an extended version

1782
01:13:49.060 --> 01:13:52.590
of that format and to do it as honestly as I could?"

1783
01:13:52.590 --> 01:13:53.766
So that's interesting.

1784
01:13:53.766 --> 01:13:56.979
That's kind of a storytelling workshop for you in a way

1785
01:13:56.979 --> 01:13:58.930
the 12-step.
Oh, big time.

1786
01:13:58.930 --> 01:14:00.080
And as a podcast host.

1787
01:14:00.080 --> 01:14:03.110
The bridge between, the writing bridge from undergraduate

1788
01:14:03.110 --> 01:14:03.990
all the way to the book.

1789
01:14:03.990 --> 01:14:05.810
But the storytelling, the creative writing,

1790
01:14:05.810 --> 01:14:09.190
the storytelling and the memoir aspect is kind of like

1791
01:14:10.190 --> 01:14:12.160
you're soaking it in in all these meetings.

1792
01:14:12.160 --> 01:14:13.890
Right, and when you are in a position

1793
01:14:13.890 --> 01:14:16.210
where you're telling your story a lot,

1794
01:14:16.210 --> 01:14:18.220
it starts to tell you what it is.

1795
